From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:04:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 189/194] hjviewer: the service waits for a refused connection
 window to go and starts its own

With the connection window's pipe checked, the service's process
(--server) refuses a pipe that is there but not served by its own
session's window: the window of another Windows session (fast user
switching), of another copy of the app, of an older version, or
someone else's. src/server/connection.rs start_ipc then started a window
as before, but that window cannot take the name while the refused one
holds it (parity-tokio-ipc makes the first instance), so it ends at
once; after 20 tries of 0.3 s the --server gave up and the connection
was closed with "connection manager error". A window whose connections
are gone closes itself about 6 s later (flutter/lib/models/server_model.dart,
12 ticks of 500 ms), which is about when the --server gave up, so an
incoming connection right after a user switch or an update could fail
where 1.9.3 showed it, in the other session's window.

When the first connect fails for any reason but a missing pipe
(ipc::is_endpoint_missing, a NotFound io error), start_ipc now tries 40
times instead of 20 (12 s) and, the first time the pipe is missing,
starts a window again, which then takes the name; the window comes up
in this session. Starting the window moved unchanged into
start_cm_process.

Left alone: when the pipe is missing at the start, and on Linux and
macOS, nothing changes (the longer wait is Windows only). A window that
stays open in another session (it keeps an ended connection with an
unanswered chat or a file transfer until its user closes it) still
holds the name: connections in this session fail after 12 s until it is
closed, where 1.9.3 showed them to that other user. The --server does
not end other windows itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
